# Caching Strategy

## Objective

Only introduce caching where there's a real, identified cost to avoid repeating — and always define how the cache gets invalidated.

## Core Principles

- Every cache needs an explicit invalidation strategy defined before it's added — a cache without invalidation is a bug generator.
- Cache at the layer where the data is actually expensive to (re)compute or fetch, not arbitrarily.
- Prefer short, well-understood TTLs over indefinite caching unless invalidation is airtight.
- Don't cache data that must always be fresh (e.g. auth/permission checks) without very deliberate justification.

## Requirements

- State the cache key, TTL/invalidation trigger, and staleness tolerance whenever adding a cache.
- Reuse the project's existing caching layer/mechanism instead of introducing a new one.
- Consider cache stampede/thundering-herd risk for high-traffic cached endpoints.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Adding a cache with no invalidation plan ('we'll figure that out later').
- Caching permission/authorization results in a way that could serve stale access decisions.
- Introducing a second caching mechanism when the project already has one (e.g. Redis) available.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Every new cache has an explicit, stated invalidation strategy.
✓ Caching is applied only where a real cost was identified, not speculatively.
✓ The existing project caching mechanism was reused, not duplicated.

## Golden Rule

A cache without a clear invalidation story isn't an optimization, it's a future bug.
